--- !u!114 &2018873943
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 2018873941}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: f4688fdb7df04437aeb418b961361dc5, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  m_Material: {fileID: 0}
  m_Color: {r: 1, g: 1, b: 1, a: 1}
  m_RaycastTarget: 1
  m_RaycastPadding: {x: 0, y: 0, z: 0, w: 0}
  m_Maskable: 1
  m_OnCullStateChanged:
    m_PersistentCalls:
      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'The Ashoka Pillars are a remarkable group of ancient stone pillars erected
    during the reign of Emperor Ashoka of the Mauryan Empire, mainly in the 3rd century
    BCE. These pillars were placed at important locations across the Indian subcontinent
    and carried messages connected with Dharma, peace, morality, and good governance.


    The
    pillars were generally made from finely polished sandstone and are famous for
    their smooth surfaces and impressive craftsmanship. A typical pillar consists
    of several major parts: the base, shaft, abacus, and capital. The tall shaft
    supports the upper decorative section, while the abacus contains symbolic carvings.


    The
    most famous example is the Lion Capital of Sarnath. It features four Asiatic
    lions standing back to back. The lions represent strength, courage, confidence,
    and power. Below them is the abacus, decorated with animals such as the elephant,
    horse, bull, and lion, along with Dharma Chakras.


    The Dharma Chakra, or
    Wheel of Dharma, represents the eternal cycle of Dharma, justice, and righteous
    living. The Ashoka Chakra from this tradition is also represented on the national
    flag of India.


    The Lion Capital of Sarnath has become one of the most important
    national symbols of India. The four lions represent India''s commitment to courage,
    strength, and responsible leadership. The Dharma Chakra represents the importance
    of righteousness and continuous progress.


    Through an AR experience, students
    can explore the pillar from different angles, identify its individual components,
    view an exploded structure, examine the Lion Capital and Dharma Chakra, and learn
    the meaning behind its symbols. This interactive approach transforms a historical
    monument into an engaging 3D learning experience.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 956b217cc7cf8c3428c64cf5f91c13b5, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: -7116138974986501691, guid: 956b217cc7cf8c3428c64cf5f91c13b5, type: 2}
  m_fontSharedMaterials: []
  m_fontMaterial: {fileID: 0}
